<BaseLayout title="About" description="Notes from Jacob Shin in Seoul on agent engineering, enterprise AX, investing, and mathematics." language="en" alternateLanguageUrl="/about/">
  <article class="prose-page shell">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1><span>Notes on real-world problems I’ve worked through,</span><br /><span>and the interests I keep exploring.</span></h1>
    </header>
    <div class="prose-grid">
      <aside>
        <p>Jacob Shin</p>
        <p>Agent Engineering / Enterprise AX</p>
        <p>Seoul</p>
        <p>Est. 2026</p>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lead">I am Jacob Shin, based in Seoul.</p>
        <p>I tend to connect the problems I encounter at work with questions I have been following for much longer. The work I go deepest on is <strong>agent engineering</strong> and <strong>enterprise AX</strong>. I also keep a sustained interest in investing and early-stage companies, while statistics, probability, and optimization remain subjects I return to whenever I am learning something new.</p>
        <p>The Archive is my personal record of the judgments and questions that build up along the way. It is less a feed designed to publish more often than a place to preserve what I saw, the hypotheses I formed, and what would make me change my mind. I explain that choice at greater length in <a href="/en/misc/essays/why-this-archive-is-open/">Why I Keep Research as an Archive, Not a Feed</a>.</p>
        <h2>What I write about</h2>
        <p><strong>Agent Engineering</strong> gathers cases drawn from problems I have worked through: why a design became more complex, which choices proved useful, and what had to be revised once a system met operations. I aim to make the lessons concrete without disclosing what should remain private.</p>
        <p><strong>Enterprise AX</strong> captures the ideas and observations that come from solving problems with internal stakeholders. I am interested not only in the technology, but in how change is received inside an organization and becomes part of an everyday workflow.</p>
        <p><strong>Public-equity investing</strong> begins with companies or positions I am genuinely following. I form the hypothesis myself, then use AI to help check sources, search for counterexamples, and repeat the validation process. The goal is not to recommend a trade, but to make the evidence and uncertainty behind a judgment visible.</p>
        <p><strong>Early- and growth-stage companies</strong> are a personal research interest. I enjoy examining what a business can grow into and how its strategy changes under real constraints.</p>
        <p><strong>Mathematics</strong> is where I connect statistics, probability, and optimization to everyday questions. I do not claim mastery; I simply enjoy the moment when a concept learned through data science becomes useful in work or ordinary life, and I write down new ideas in the form I understand them.</p>
        <h2>How I write</h2>
        <p>I use AI actively in the writing process, but choosing the problem and forming the hypothesis remain my work. AI is closer to a research partner: checking material, looking for counterexamples, and helping refine structure and expression. I start with a question, explain it, test it against an example or model, and write only what I can support.</p>
        <div class="method-signature" aria-label="Writing method">
          <strong>Writing method</strong>
          <em>Explain → Model → Test → Interpret.</em>
        </div>
        <p>Korean is the source edition. The English pages are edited from the same argument and evidence for a natural English reading experience, not translated line by line. I do not bury mistakes; when a view changes, I leave the reason in the text.</p>
        <h2>Contact Jacob Shin</h2>
        <p>If you have a different reading of an article, a challenge, a question, or a better source, email <a href="mailto:yunchae.cfa@gmail.com">yunchae.cfa@gmail.com</a>. I welcome conversations worth thinking through together.</p>
        <div class="about-notes">
          <p>Investing pieces are for research and record-keeping. They are not investment advice. Judgment and risk stay with the reader.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
